Theorem IA-T5: Frame-limited class-ledger robustness under tier-1 edge introduction

  Preconditions:
    - The T2 CFG-ledger model is the substrate: per-partition-class event
      ledgers derived from instruction-shaped input by the computed
      classification; the three-clause per-class per-path invariant; the
      partition taken AS GIVEN input (the T1 union-find representatives).
    - A NEW tier-1 same-allocation edge enters the T1 partition only
      through the admission rule: equal birth-sites admit it
      (PartitionAdm.tier1), so samerep_birthsite_sound keeps every merge
      birth-site-sound. At the class-map level the T1 PartitionUF.union is
      a ROOT REDIRECT (point the retired representative at the absorbing
      one); mergeClasses is that redirect - members of the retired class
      re-map to the absorbing class, every other value keeps its class -
      witnessed computationally on a concrete union-find over the touched
      value universe (the T1 Part-C discipline: representatives COMPUTED
      through the real fuelled find/union, never hardcoded).
    - The derived ledger depends on the classifier ONLY through the
      membership test (derivation congruence, the jump-arg partition
      routing and the suffix-computed dynamic-COW sibling floor included).
    - The compiled Lean model is the calculus authority; conformance of the
      shipped emitter to the model is established empirically on the
      compiler's ARC-verified test surface, not discharged here.

  Soundness property:
    Introducing a new tier-1 partition edge is FRAME-LIMITED. (frame) Every
    class whose membership is untouched - any class distinct from both
    merge participants - derives a VERBATIM-identical ledger on every
    instruction stream: same event list, same three-clause verdict, same
    balanced-and-safe status; the retired class derives the empty ledger.
    (bounded impact) The merged class's ledger is exactly the positional
    interleaving of the two prior classes' events - at every instruction,
    the event that instruction contributed to whichever prior class it
    touched - on the mutate-free non-bridging fragment; the one folding
    shape is stated honestly: a jump-arg handoff bridging the two merge
    participants was a cross-class consume/credit pair and collapses into
    the RL-4 same-class exemption, net-preservingly. (robustness) Clause 1
    composes UNCONDITIONALLY: the merged net is the sum of the prior nets
    on EVERY stream, so two zero-net classes merge to a zero-net class.
    Clauses 2/3 compose under two honest side conditions, EACH proven
    necessary by a negative witness: (a) running-nonnegativity - each
    prior class's running count stays >= 0 at every prefix; without it two
    individually-clause-satisfying ledgers interleave into a READ-at-0
    use-after-free (the three clauses are silent on an interior negative
    dip absent reads); (b) mutate-freeness - the dynamic-COW sibling floor
    is computed from the path suffix and the merge can RAISE it (a
    formerly-cross-class suffix reader becomes a live sibling) past a
    running count that satisfied the pre-merge floor; clause-3 preservation
    genuinely requires re-deriving sibling floors at the merged partition.
    An unconditional clause-2/3 preservation claim is FALSE and provably
    rejected. This grounds why incremental tier-1-edge introduction cannot
    silently break distant classes: every perturbation is confined to the
    merged classes, and there it is exactly characterized.

  Proof obligation:
    (P1) The redirect and its T1 grounding. Untouched-class membership is
         verbatim under mergeClasses; merged-class membership is the
         disjunction of the two prior memberships; the retired class is
         empty. The introduced semantic edge is a PartitionAdm.tier1
         admission (equal birth-sites). On the concrete witness union-find
         (a tier-1 view pair {900, 901}; singletons 902-904) the REAL
         PartitionUF.union folding the new edge (902, 900) computes exactly
         the mergeClasses redirect on every touched value; the merge is
         genuine (distinct representatives before, one after) and the
         distant class 903 keeps its representative verbatim.
    (P2) THE frame theorem. Derivation congruence (pointwise-equal
         membership tests derive verbatim-identical ledgers, by induction
         over the computed classification - the sibling-count congruence
         included) instantiated at the redirect: for every class distinct
         from both merge participants and EVERY instruction stream, the
         post-merge derived ledger IS the pre-merge derived ledger, hence
         the three-clause verdict and the balanced-and-safe status are
         unchanged verbatim. The retired class derives the empty ledger.
         Instantiated at the concrete witness for the distant class.
    (P3) Net additivity - clause 1 unconditional. The per-instruction net
         contribution to the merged class equals the SUM of the two prior
         contributions (every instruction, the bridging jump-arg rows
         summing -1 + 1 = 0), so the merged class's net ledger is the sum
         of the prior nets on every stream, and two zero-net prior classes
         merge to a zero-net class.
    (P4) Bounded impact - the positional interleave. On a mutate-free
         stream with no jump-arg bridging the two merge participants, the
         merged class's derived ledger equals the filterMap of the
         per-instruction pair image: at every instruction, the event it
         contributed to whichever prior class it touched (at most one, by
         disjointness). The bridging-collapse lemma states the one folding
         shape: source in the retired class, receiver in the absorbing
         class - a cross-class handoff (consume/credit, the committed
         jump_arg_cross_class_handoff) before the merge, the RL-4
         exemption (no event) after it, net-preserving.
    (P5) Robustness - clauses 2/3 under the honest side conditions. The
         floors-composition lemma threads both prior running counts: the
         merged count at every prefix is their sum; a firing event's floor
         is met by its own class's floor plus the OTHER class's
         nonnegativity; a bridging handoff moves one unit between the
         counts leaving the sum and the merged ledger untouched. Corollary:
         both prior classes three-clause-satisfying + both running counts
         never negative + mutate-free stream => the merged class satisfies
         all three clauses.
    (P6) Negative witnesses - the side conditions are necessary. READ-at-0:
         on the computed identity-class witness, both prior classes satisfy
         the three clauses (one carries an interior consume-before-birth
         dip - net zero, floors silent), the running-nonnegativity check
         pinpoints the violated guard, and the merged class reads at count
         0 - three clauses false, the operational use-after-free flag set,
         while the merged net STILL zeroes (the clause-1 slice really is
         unconditional). Merge-raised sibling floor: both prior classes
         satisfy the clauses AND both counts stay nonnegative, yet the
         merged dynamic-COW mutate faces floor 2 at count 1 (the
         formerly-cross-class suffix reader became a live sibling) - three
         clauses false, the operational COW-hazard flag set; mutate-freeness
         cannot be dropped.

